so Martin your film is the opening night  film at sci-fi london film  year tell us what that felt like  fantastic my films called death it was  great I mean we had already had the cast  and crew screening a few days before  which was hectic because I was doing all  the behind-the-scenes bringing taxis but  this was great because all i have to do  is turn up and magically my film appears  on the screen i have to have to do a  little speech and then we had a  wonderful Q&amp;A with all my actors  afterwards it was fantastic and we sold  out so they had to book another  screening just to accommodate the demand  to see my wonderful film brilliant well  why don't you tell us a little bit about  death and why everyone was flocking to  it very few people actually say what  they're going to do especially in the  film industry or do what they can do  what they say they're going to do and I  still going to make the film and it's  taken two years and we did it and we've  done it and we've made a small film but  it's got famous people and it's got  Leslie Phillips it's got Paul Freeman  from Raiders of the Lost Ark nick moran  from lock stock two smoking barrels and  harry potter and we've got Mike mikkel  from emmerdale and doctors and Sarah  Jane done from Hollyoaks and Lionel from  Moulin Rouge and EastEnders so we got  all sorts of faces you constantly going  through the film going oh I know him and  around those wonderful actors we put a  skeleton in the middle which is a really  nice story about a family coming back  together after their last surviving  parent has died their dad and they all  come back together and they haven't been  there at home for seven years something  bad happened and sent them away and  we're finding them coming back for the  first time and they have to face each  other face the future and more  importantly face the past see what was  the inspiration for the story then  there's lots of things I i had a year  where five people I knew died which was  very annoying so but I thought you know  we all have to deal with death and what  happens after death and clearing houses  and meeting up with family members you  may not like that you don't want to  speak to you but you have to deal with  and I thought this is something  everyone's got to deal with them we will  all die  and one of the posters I was going to  have it was saying death coming to you  eventually but I thought people would  get upset about that so we got rid of  that you know we all have to deal with  the various things have to do with death  and bereavement and I thought let's deal  with this I and also when I was a kid I  was convinced i saw a ghost and I felt  these things come together so those  moments come together and you know I  wanted to make a film that no one else  was making I coulda done zombies I could  have done horror could have done  gangsters could on East End gangsters  could have done depressing kitchen sink  housing estate thing but I thought I  wanted to make a film that only I can  make so my that's my voice you know it's  a work of art just like a song or a  painting this is the Martin gooch film  I've done telling I've directed  Hollyoaks and doctors all those things  so we know technically I can direct  that's a fine let's do the thing I want  to do that nobody else can do and death  is the result now you mentioned TV there  and having two Hollyoaks and doctors and  all those sort of things what how  difficult it moving from TV into making  independent films well I started off as  a runner and then I was a clapper loader  and a focus pillar for a very long time  I did 13 feature films as a technician  as a cameraman folks pillar and a camera  operator so I had a very very solid  grounding in how to shoot so I didn't  find it difficult what was difficult is  getting people interested and even  having made the film getting people to  watch the film that's the key because  for us is two years work but for  everyone else it's 100 minutes of just  sitting down going well that's quite  nice thank you very much let's go and  watch The Avengers you mentioned it took  around two years to make the film what  kept you going throughout that process  because two years is quite a long time  out of your life isn't it to making a  film yeah well I mean you know I love  making films all I've ever wanted to do  it's what I do for a living so I had to  prove that I could make a feature film  and I've spent I'd spent a very long  time more than a decade trying to raise  money to make the film no give me any  money and there was a moment where I  thought you know I could spend another  10 years  trying to get money or I could sit down  and make a film and prove that I've done  it and we've done it we've made a film  and you know it's a great film it's a  nice little someone said it was  beautifully British someone that else  said Robert Rankin the famous science  fiction author said it was a magnificent  achievement you know and these people's  opinions I respect and we've made a  great little film we're going to send it  to the festival's see where how well it  does what business it does and hopefully  generate interest so we can do it all  over again tell me a little bit about  the challenges that you faced along  those two years Martin oh it's money  it's all finance I mean if someone turns  up and says here's a hundred thousand  pounds you can budget and plan it and  say right we're going to spend this much  money on this things we didn't have that  when i started the film we had 300 quid  which is a nothing and money came in  3,000 pounds came in here at one point  five thousand came in and then 250 and  money sort of comes in waves and as it  comes in you have to work out what you  can spend on things i mean we don't know  any money which is fantastic it's not a  credit card film but the challenges are  just keep going because you get to a  point where all are all our sound came  in one day and I and 